# --- Unity ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Library is 7.3 GB and ENTIRELY derived: Unity rebuilds it from Assets +
# ProjectSettings. Temp/Logs/UserSettings are the same.
unity/Library/
unity/Temp/
unity/Logs/
unity/UserSettings/
unity/Build/
unity/obj/
unity/.utmp/
unity/.gradle/
unity/.vs/
unity/*.csproj
unity/*.sln
*.unityproxy

# --- Build output ----------------------------------------------------------
# build/ is 757 MB and holds a 90 MB APK; GitHub's file limit is 100 MB.
# Both are reproducible.
build/
aab/

# --- .NET ------------------------------------------------------------------
[Bb]in/
[Oo]bj/

# --- Python ----------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# --- Render ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Working renders are derived and remade on every tour.
# The STORE images are the exception: they are a release asset (docs/44).
render/*
# THE NEGATION HAD TO MOVE WITH THE FOLDER. The English rename moved
# render/magaza to render/store and this line did not, so for the last day
# render/* has ignored everything and the un-ignore has pointed at a folder
# that no longer exists. The store images - the ones the comment above calls
# a release asset - simply stopped being committed, silently, because an
# ignore rule fails by doing nothing.
# THE README'S OWN PICTURES.
#
# Everything else under render/ is regenerated on every run and rightly
# ignored, but the README is the first page anybody sees and its images have
# to be IN the repository or they are broken links on GitHub. These are
# copies, taken by hand from the generated set when the README is updated -
# which is the point: they change when somebody decides the front page should
# change, not when a verification run happens to fire.
!render/readme/
!render/readme/*.png

# THE EVIDENCE CROPS ARE NOT OUTPUT, THEY ARE THE RECORD.
#
# render/ is regenerated on every run and rightly ignored. render/zoom/ is
# different: these are hand-taken crops of a frame that showed a defect, and
# the comments that cite them ("the foam went on floating in a hole - see
# render/zoom/before-empty-basin.png") are pointing at a file a reader has to
# be able to open. A comment referring to something outside the repository is
# a comment that will be wrong within a month.
!render/zoom/
!render/zoom/*.png

!render/store/
# The per-cuisine and per-language folders only. A bare render/store/*.png is
# the OLD flat layout - every store run used to overwrite it, so what sat
# there was whichever cuisine ran last with nothing in the name to say which.
render/store/*.png

# --- Operating system ------------------------------------------------------
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.DS_Store

# Unity's Performance Testing package writes these into Assets/Resources on
# every run that loads the test framework, and deletes them again on the next
# one. They got swept into a commit by a `git add -A` taken while a tour was
# running, and the next tour deleted them - which is how a generated file
# announces that it does not belong in the repository.
unity/Assets/Resources/PerformanceTestRunInfo.json
unity/Assets/Resources/PerformanceTestRunInfo.json.meta
unity/Assets/Resources/PerformanceTestRunSettings.json
unity/Assets/Resources/PerformanceTestRunSettings.json.meta

# The room-layout captures are DIAGNOSTIC OUTPUT, not source.
#
# Editor/RoomLayout.Capture writes sixteen timestamped PNGs per run - one per
# tier per framing - and every one of them was being committed. 308 of them
# were in the tree by 18 September, 6.6 MB, and check.py runs the tool, so the
# repository grew every time anybody verified anything. Nobody has ever opened
# a capture from three weeks ago: the one that matters is the one on disk now.
#
# The ones already committed are left alone - rewriting history to reclaim
# 6.6 MB is not worth what it costs everybody with a clone - but no new one
# joins them.
tools/art/out/unity/floor_*_*.png

